    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit, rejecting the creation of an owner with **HTTP 429** once 100 or more owners already carry today's `registrationDate`. I followed the repository's existing per-city-capacity pattern exactly.\n\nThree changes:\n\n1. **`OwnerDailyLimitExceededException`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` carrying the `date` and `limit`, modeled on `OwnerCityCapacityExceededException`.\n\n2. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` for the new exception that maps it to `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` (429), building the same `ProblemDetail` shape as the sibling handlers with `errors: [\"registrationDate\"]`.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1`** \u2014 added a `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY = 100` constant and a `rejectDailyLimitExceeded()` method (invoked early in `addOwner`, alongside the existing city-capacity check). It counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals `LocalDate.now()` and throws when the count is `>= 100`.\n\nThe check sits before the owner is saved, so the 101st create in a day is rejected while the first 100 succeed \u2014 matching the `coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` acceptance test (create 100, then expect the next to return 429).",
